cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  AF2-PocketCross-DTA: does real protein structure improve
  drug–target affinity prediction for unseen targets?
message: >-
  If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using
  the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Turjo
    family-names: Rahman
    email: us.khan.2002@gmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/TurjoRahman-afk/Drug"
abstract: >-
  A drug–target affinity model evaluated on the cold-protein
  (unseen-target) split of the DAVIS kinase benchmark, where all
  test proteins are held out of training. Protein graphs are built
  from AlphaFold2 Cα contact topology rather than sequence-predicted
  contacts, and drug atoms attend over protein residues with the
  attention biased by a residue prior recycled from the protein
  GNN's own per-residue embeddings.

  Across three split seeds the model reaches CI 0.8541 ± 0.0053,
  second of nine published methods and statistically close to the
  best, while absolute accuracy (MSE 0.3618 ± 0.0109) does not
  improve on methods using sequence-predicted contacts. The work
  also reports a previously undocumented flaw in the DAVIS
  benchmark: 442 target entries share only 379 unique sequences,
  making 18.9% of training pairs mutually contradictory and 11.4%
  of cold-protein test pairs unlearnable by construction.
keywords:
  - drug-target affinity
  - drug discovery
  - AlphaFold2
  - protein language models
  - graph neural networks
  - Kolmogorov-Arnold networks
  - benchmark integrity
  - cold-start generalisation
license: MIT
